page ref_ws_machine {
  title = "wscript API: Machine"
  chapter_header "wscript API: Machine" {
    kicker       = "Reference · reference"
    reading_time = "30 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    This chapter is the method-by-method reference for `Machine`, the handle
    a [Lab](ref_ws_lab) gives out for a VM or a container. There is one
    handle for both kinds. Every method exists on every machine; what a
    particular machine cannot do is reported when the method is called, and
    the error names the missing capability rather than the machine's kind. A
    container with no display fails `screenshot` with
    "machine `web` has no display", never with "no such method". The
    [scripting guide](guide_scripting) explains how scripts reach a machine
    and the [vision guide](guide_vision) explains the screen methods in
    depth.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    The methods fall into groups. Lifecycle and state: `name`, `kind`,
    `start`, `stop`, `stop_force`, `restart`, `poweroff`, `state`,
    `is_ready`, `wait_ready`, `is_healthy`, `agent_answering`,
    `wait_shutdown`, `ip`, `ip_nic`. Snapshots: `snapshot`, `restore`,
    `restore_discarding_workspace`, `snapshots`, `delete_snapshot`. Input:
    `send_keys`, `type_text`, `type_text_paced`, `mouse_move`, `mouse_click`,
    `mouse_drag`. Screen: `screenshot`, `wait_for_image`,
    `wait_for_image_opts`, `wait_for_any`, `find_image`, `ocr`, `ocr_region`,
    `wait_for_text`. Guest agent: `exec`, `exec_timeout`, `copy_to`,
    `copy_from`, `logs`, `terminal`, `stats`. Identity: `logins`, `as_login`,
    `as_account`.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Two conventions apply throughout. Relative host paths, for reference
    images, screenshots and file transfers, resolve against the directory the
    running script lives in, so a provision can ship its reference crops
    beside itself. And every method that can fail returns a wscript `Result`
    with a string error; the descriptions below say what the error carries.
    The signatures use the parameter names the host registers; the generated
    interface file spells them `a0`, `a1` and so on.
  DOC

  callout "Readiness inside a first-boot script" { kind = :note body = <<DOC
    On the handle `lab.this_vm()` returns inside a template's first-boot
    provision, `is_ready` and `wait_ready` mean "the agent answers right
    now", because full readiness is deferred until that script returns. On
    every other handle they mean full readiness.
  DOC
  }

  h2 "Machine.name"
  p "The machine's name, as the lab file declares it."
  code wscript { source = "fn name(self) -> string" }
  p "It never fails."
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    for m in lab.machines() { lab.log(m.name()) }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.kind"
  p "Which kind of machine this is."
  code wscript { source = "fn kind(self) -> string" }
  p <<DOC
    Returns `vm` or `container`. It never fails. Branch on a capability error
    rather than on the kind where you can; see [lab containers](guide_containers).
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.machine("web") else { return }
    lab.log(m.name() + " is a " + m.kind())
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.start"
  p "Start the machine if it is stopped."
  code wscript { source = "fn start(self) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    A machine that is not stopped returns `Ok` without doing anything. A
    pending template or image download is completed first. The call returns
    once the emulator is running; it does not wait for readiness, so follow it
    with `Machine.wait_ready`. The error carries the reason the download or
    the boot failed.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.machine("api") else { return }
    let s = m.start()
    let r = m.wait_ready(300)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.stop"
  p "Stop the machine gracefully."
  code wscript { source = "fn stop(self) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Runs the stop ladder: for a VM an agent-requested shutdown, then an ACPI
    power-down, then a hard kill, each rung with its own timeout. For a
    container the ladder is an in-guest stop signal with a grace period, then
    an agent shutdown, then a hard kill. A machine that is already stopped
    returns `Ok`. The error carries what the ladder reported.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    match m.stop() {
        Ok(_) => lab.log("stopped"),
        Err(e) => lab.log("stop failed: " + e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.stop_force"
  p "Kill the machine immediately."
  code wscript { source = "fn stop_force(self) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Skips the ladder and sends the emulator a kill. Writes the guest had not
    flushed are lost. Use `Machine.poweroff` for a guest that has no ACPI
    and needs its disk sealed cleanly.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.container("web") else { return }
    let r = m.stop_force()
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.restart"
  p "Stop the machine gracefully, wait for it to settle, and start it again."
  code wscript { source = "fn restart(self) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Runs `Machine.stop`, waits up to sixty seconds for the power state to
    reach stopped, then runs `Machine.start`. It fails with
    "<name> did not stop for restart" when the machine does not settle in
    time, and otherwise with the stop or start error. This restarts the
    emulator; a reboot the guest performs itself is watched with
    `Machine.agent_answering` instead.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.vm("member") else { return }
    let r = m.restart()
    let ready = m.wait_ready(600)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.poweroff"
  p "Exit the emulator cleanly, flushing disk caches first."
  code wscript { source = "fn poweroff(self) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Sends the emulator a clean quit and waits for the power state to settle.
    Unlike `Machine.stop_force`, block-device caches are flushed before the
    process exits, so this is the only safe way to seal a consistent disk for
    a guest with no ACPI, such as DOS or Windows 3.x, where the ladder's last
    rung would otherwise drop unflushed writes. The error carries the reason
    the machine did not settle.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.vm("dos") else { return }
    let r = m.poweroff()
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.state"
  p "The machine's power state."
  code wscript { source = "fn state(self) -> string" }
  p <<DOC
    Returns `stopped`, `starting`, `running` or `stopping`. It never fails.
    Running says nothing about readiness; see `Machine.is_ready`.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.machine("api") else { return }
    if m.state() == "stopped" { let s = m.start() }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.is_ready"
  p "Whether the machine is fully usable."
  code wscript { source = "fn is_ready(self) -> bool" }
  p <<DOC
    Ready means the agent has come up and any first-boot work has completed.
    The flag is sticky: it stays set while the emulator runs, even through a
    reboot the guest performs itself. Use `Machine.agent_answering` for a
    live signal. Inside the machine's own first-boot provision this returns
    the live agent probe instead, as the note at the top says. It never fails.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.machine("api") else { return }
    if !m.is_ready() { let r = m.wait_ready(300) }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.wait_ready"
  p "Block until the machine is ready or the timeout passes."
  code wscript { source = "fn wait_ready(self, timeout_secs: int) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`timeout_secs`" | "int" | "How long to wait, in seconds. A negative value is treated as zero." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Polls `Machine.is_ready` with the same first-boot gating. It fails with
    "<name>: not ready after <timeout>" when the deadline passes, and with
    "<name> stopped while waiting for ready" when the machine stops in the
    meantime.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    match dc.wait_ready(600) {
        Ok(_) => lab.log("dc01 ready"),
        Err(e) => lab.log("not ready: " + e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.is_healthy"
  p "The latest verdict of the machine's healthcheck."
  code wscript { source = "fn is_healthy(self) -> bool" }
  p <<DOC
    A machine that declares no healthcheck counts as healthy once it is
    ready, so a script can gate on this for every machine. `vmlab machine
    capabilities` says whether the machine has a healthcheck at all. It never
    fails.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(web) = lab.container("web") else { return }
    if !web.is_healthy() { lab.log("web is unhealthy") }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.agent_answering"
  p "Whether the guest agent answers a ping right now."
  code wscript { source = "fn agent_answering(self) -> bool" }
  p <<DOC
    The live probe, on every handle. It goes false while the guest is down
    or mid-reboot even though `Machine.is_ready` stays true, which is what a
    build provision needs to watch a reboot it asked the guest to perform.
    It never fails.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let r = m.exec("shutdown", ["/r", "/t", "0"])
    while m.agent_answering() { vmlab::sleep_ms(1000) }
    while !m.agent_answering() { vmlab::sleep_ms(2000) }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.wait_shutdown"
  p "Block until the machine's power state is stopped."
  code wscript { source = "fn wait_shutdown(self, timeout_secs: int) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`timeout_secs`" | "int" | "How long to wait, in seconds." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Waits for the exit monitor to settle the state at stopped, however the
    machine got there: a `Machine.stop`, a guest-initiated shutdown, or a
    crash. The error says the state was not reached in time.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let r = m.exec("shutdown", ["/s", "/t", "0"])
    let down = m.wait_shutdown(120)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.ip"
  p "The machine's first IPv4 address."
  code wscript { source = "fn ip(self) -> Result[string, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Asks the agent for its interfaces, matches them to the lab file's NIC
    order by MAC address, and returns the first NIC that has an IPv4 address.
    It fails when the agent is not reachable and with
    "<name>: no IPv4 address reported by agent" when no NIC has one yet.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.machine("api") else { return }
    match m.ip() {
        Ok(ip) => lab.log("api at " + ip),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.ip_nic"
  p "The IPv4 address of one NIC, by index."
  code wscript { source = "fn ip_nic(self, nic: int) -> Result[string, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`nic`" | "int" | "The NIC's position among the machine's `nic {}` blocks, starting at 0. A negative value is treated as 0." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The same lookup as `Machine.ip`, restricted to one NIC. It fails with
    the same messages, and the "no IPv4 address" error also covers an index
    beyond the last NIC.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.vm("router") else { return }
    let inside = m.ip_nic(1)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.snapshot"
  p "Take a snapshot, online if the machine is running and offline if not."
  code wscript { source = "fn snapshot(self, name: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "The snapshot's name." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    A running machine gets an online snapshot that captures memory and
    resumes exactly where it was on restore; a stopped one gets an offline
    disk snapshot. A container's record also pins the image digest the
    capture is valid against. The record is written to the lab's state file
    and a `snapshot.created` event is emitted; see [Events](ref_events).
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    On a dev machine the [workspace syncer](guide_dev) flushes first, so the
    snapshot is coherent with the canonical tree. If the guest holds work the
    host has never seen, the call refuses, and there is no flag to override
    it. The error carries that refusal, the pin failure, or the emulator's own
    error. Snapshots are not a workspace backup; see [Snapshots](guide_snapshots).
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    match dc.snapshot("post-promote") {
        Ok(_) => lab.log("snapshot taken"),
        Err(e) => lab.log("snapshot refused: " + e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.restore"
  p "Restore a snapshot by name."
  code wscript { source = "fn restore(self, name: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "The snapshot to restore." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    An online snapshot resumes running where it was; an offline one leaves
    the machine off. A container's pinned image digest is checked first, and
    a mismatch fails with a message naming both digests and the two ways
    out: destroy the machine or restore the original pin. It fails with
    "<name> has no snapshot <snap>" for an unknown name.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    On a dev machine the syncer is taken off the workspace before the rewind
    and put back owing a re-seed, which carries the canonical host copy back
    into the guest and lets nothing flow the other way. A workspace that is
    halted on a conflict refuses to restore, because the rewind would destroy
    the guest copy of every conflicting path; that refusal is the one
    `Machine.restore_discarding_workspace` overrides.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let r = dc.restore("post-promote")
    let ready = dc.wait_ready(300)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.restore_discarding_workspace"
  p "Restore a snapshot on a dev machine whose workspace is halted, discarding the guest copy of every conflicting path."
  code wscript { source = "fn restore_discarding_workspace(self, name: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "The snapshot to restore." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The same restore as `Machine.restore` with the explicit discard flag
    set, the counterpart of `vmlab snapshot restore --discard-guest-changes`.
    It is a separate verb rather than a boolean argument so that the
    destruction is asked for by name at the call site. On a machine without
    a workspace the flag means nothing and the call behaves as
    `Machine.restore`. The errors are those of `Machine.restore` minus the
    halt refusal.
  DOC
  callout "This destroys guest work" { kind = :warning body = <<DOC
    Guest-side changes at every halted path are lost and are not recoverable
    from vmlab. Resolve the halt with `vmlab dev sync resolve` first if any
    of them matter.
  DOC
  }
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dev) = lab.vm("dev") else { return }
    let r = dev.restore_discarding_workspace("clean")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.snapshots"
  p "The names of the machine's snapshots."
  code wscript { source = "fn snapshots(self) -> Result[List[string], string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Returns the names recorded in the lab's state file. The full records,
    with when each was taken and whether it is online, are what
    `vmlab snapshot list` prints. It fails when the machine does not exist.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    match m.snapshots() {
        Ok(names) => { for n in names { lab.log(n) } }
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.delete_snapshot"
  p "Delete a snapshot by name."
  code wscript { source = "fn delete_snapshot(self, name: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`name`" | "string" | "The snapshot to delete." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Removes the snapshot from the disk and its record from the state file.
    No event is emitted. The error carries the emulator's or the image
    tool's reason.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let r = m.delete_snapshot("scratch")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.send_keys"
  p "Send one key chord to the machine's display."
  code wscript { source = "fn send_keys(self, chord: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`chord`" | "string" | "Key names joined with `-`, pressed together, for example `ctrl-alt-del` or `f2`." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Key names are the emulator's own key codes with common aliases: `ctrl`,
    `alt`, `shift`, `win`, `enter`, `esc`, `tab`, `space`, `backspace`,
    `del`, `up`, `down`, `left`, `right`, `home`, `end`, `pageup`,
    `pagedown`, `insert`, `f1` to `f12`, and any single letter or digit.
    Names are case-insensitive. It fails with "machine <name> has no
    display" on a machine without one, with "unknown key <name> in chord"
    for a name it does not know, and with "empty key chord" for an empty
    string.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let k = dc.send_keys("ctrl-alt-del")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.type_text"
  p "Type literal text into the machine's display, one character at a time."
  code wscript { source = "fn type_text(self, text: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`text`" | "string" | "The characters to type. A newline is typed as Enter." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Uses a US keyboard layout and pauses 35 milliseconds between characters.
    Characters the layout cannot produce fail with a message naming the
    character. It fails on a machine without a display.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let t = dc.type_text("Password1!\n")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.type_text_paced"
  p "Type literal text with a chosen pause between characters."
  code wscript { source = "fn type_text_paced(self, text: string, delay_ms: int) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`text`" | "string" | "The characters to type." |
      | "`delay_ms`" | "int" | "The pause between characters, in milliseconds. A negative value is treated as 0." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    `Machine.type_text` with the pacing exposed, for a guest that drops
    keystrokes at the default pace. The errors are the same.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(old) = lab.vm("win98") else { return }
    let t = old.type_text_paced("setup.exe\n", 120)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.mouse_move"
  p "Move the pointer to an absolute position on the display."
  code wscript { source = "fn mouse_move(self, x: int, y: int) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`x`" | "int" | "Horizontal position in framebuffer pixels, from the left." |
      | "`y`" | "int" | "Vertical position in framebuffer pixels, from the top." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The position is remembered on the handle, and `Machine.mouse_click`
    reuses it. The handle returned by `Machine.as_login` shares the same
    pointer, since it is the same machine. It fails on a machine without a
    display.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let Ok(m) = dc.wait_for_image("images/ok-button.png", 60) else { return }
    let mv = dc.mouse_move(m.cx, m.cy)
    let cl = dc.mouse_click("left")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.mouse_click"
  p "Click a mouse button at the position the last move set."
  code wscript { source = "fn mouse_click(self, button: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`button`" | "string" | "`left`, `middle` or `right`." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Presses and releases the button with a short pause between, at the
    position the preceding `Machine.mouse_move` or `Machine.mouse_drag`
    left the pointer; with no move yet the position is (0, 0). It fails on a
    machine without a display and on an unknown button name.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let mv = dc.mouse_move(640, 400)
    let cl = dc.mouse_click("right")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.mouse_drag"
  p "Press the left button at one point, drag to another, and release."
  code wscript { source = "fn mouse_drag(self, x1: int, y1: int, x2: int, y2: int) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`x1`" | "int" | "Where to press, horizontal." |
      | "`y1`" | "int" | "Where to press, vertical." |
      | "`x2`" | "int" | "Where to release, horizontal." |
      | "`y2`" | "int" | "Where to release, vertical." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The pointer moves in a few steps between the two points so the guest
    sees a drag rather than a jump, and is left at the release point. It
    fails on a machine without a display.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let d = dc.mouse_drag(100, 100, 400, 300)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.screenshot"
  p "Save a PNG of the machine's display and return where it was written."
  code wscript { source = "fn screenshot(self, path: string) -> Result[string, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`path`" | "string" | "The host file to write. Relative paths resolve against the script's directory. Empty picks a timestamped name under the lab's `.vmlab/screenshots/`." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Returns the path written. Missing parent directories are created. With
    an empty `path` the file is named `<machine>-<UTC timestamp>.png`. It
    fails on a machine without a display and with the write error otherwise.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn handle(event: Event, lab: Lab) {
    if event.name != "vm.crashed" { return }
    let Ok(m) = lab.machine(event.vm) else { return }
    match m.screenshot("") {
        Ok(path) => lab.log("saved " + path),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.wait_for_image"
  p "Wait until a reference image appears on the display."
  code wscript { source = "fn wait_for_image(self, image: string, timeout_secs: int) -> Result[Match, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`image`" | "string" | "A PNG to look for. Relative paths resolve against the script's directory; by convention they live in `images/`." |
      | "`timeout_secs`" | "int" | "How long to keep looking, in seconds." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Grabs the screen once a second and runs normalised template matching
    over the whole of it with a similarity threshold of 0.9. Returns the
    first [Match](ref_ws_types), whose `cx` and `cy` anchor a click. It fails
    with "timed out after <n>s waiting for [<image>] on <name>" when the
    deadline passes, with "reference image <path>: <reason>" when the file
    cannot be loaded, and on a machine without a display.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    match dc.wait_for_image("images/login.png", 120) {
        Ok(m) => { let mv = dc.mouse_move(m.cx, m.cy) },
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.wait_for_image_opts"
  p "`Machine.wait_for_image` with the threshold and search region exposed."
  code wscript { source = "fn wait_for_image_opts(self, image: string, timeout_secs: int, threshold: float, region: List[int]) -> Result[Match, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`image`" | "string" | "The reference PNG." |
      | "`timeout_secs`" | "int" | "How long to keep looking, in seconds." |
      | "`threshold`" | "float" | "The minimum similarity score, 0 to 1. The default elsewhere is 0.9." |
      | "`region`" | "List[int]" | "`[x, y, w, h]` to search only part of the screen, or `[]` for the whole screen." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Behaves as `Machine.wait_for_image` otherwise. It also fails with
    "region needs [x, y, w, h], got <n> elements" when `region` has any
    other length. Restricting the region makes a match faster and keeps a
    small crop from matching in the wrong place.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let m = dc.wait_for_image_opts("images/tray-icon.png", 60, 0.8, [1700, 1000, 220, 80])
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.wait_for_any"
  p "Wait until any one of several reference images appears."
  code wscript { source = "fn wait_for_any(self, images: List[string], timeout_secs: int) -> Result[Match, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`images`" | "List[string]" | "The reference PNGs, tried in order on every grab." |
      | "`timeout_secs`" | "int" | "How long to keep looking, in seconds." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    On each grab the images are tried in list order and the first match
    wins, at the default threshold over the whole screen. The returned
    `Match` does not say which image matched; compare its position if that
    matters. The errors are those of `Machine.wait_for_image`, with the
    whole list named in the timeout message.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let m = dc.wait_for_any(["images/desktop.png", "images/oobe.png"], 300)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.find_image"
  p "Look for a reference image once, without waiting."
  code wscript { source = "fn find_image(self, image: string) -> Result[Option[Match], string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`image`" | "string" | "The reference PNG." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    One grab, one match at the default threshold over the whole screen.
    Returns `None` when nothing scores high enough; that is not an error.
    It fails when the reference cannot be loaded and on a machine without a
    display.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    match dc.find_image("images/update-prompt.png") {
        Ok(Some(m)) => { let mv = dc.mouse_move(m.cx, m.cy) let cl = dc.mouse_click("left") }
        Ok(None) => lab.log("no prompt"),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.ocr"
  p "Read the text on the whole display."
  code wscript { source = "fn ocr(self) -> Result[string, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Grabs the screen and runs it through the OCR engine. The result is the
    recognised text in reading order, with the engine's line breaks. It
    fails on a machine without a display and with the engine's error
    otherwise. See the [vision guide](guide_vision) for what OCR does and
    does not read well.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    match dc.ocr() {
        Ok(text) => lab.log(text),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.ocr_region"
  p "Read the text in one region of the display."
  code wscript { source = "fn ocr_region(self, region: List[int]) -> Result[string, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`region`" | "List[int]" | "`[x, y, w, h]` in framebuffer pixels, or `[]` for the whole screen." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    `Machine.ocr` restricted to a rectangle. It also fails with
    "region needs [x, y, w, h], got <n> elements" for any other list length.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let title = dc.ocr_region([0, 0, 1024, 40])
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.wait_for_text"
  p "Wait until OCR of the display matches a regular expression."
  code wscript { source = "fn wait_for_text(self, pattern: string, timeout_secs: int) -> Result[Match, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`pattern`" | "string" | "A regular expression, matched anywhere in the recognised text." |
      | "`timeout_secs`" | "int" | "How long to keep looking, in seconds." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Grabs and OCRs the whole screen once a second. On a match it returns a
    [Match](ref_ws_types) whose `text` field is the matched text; the
    position fields are 0 and the score is 1, because OCR does not report
    where the words were. It fails with "bad pattern: <reason>" for an
    invalid expression, with "timed out after <n>s waiting for /<pattern>/
    on <name>" at the deadline, and on a machine without a display.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    match dc.wait_for_text("Press Ctrl.Alt.Del", 300) {
        Ok(m) => lab.log("saw: " + m.text),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.exec"
  p "Run a command in the guest through the agent and collect its output."
  code wscript { source = "fn exec(self, cmd: string, args: List[string]) -> Result[ExecResult, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`cmd`" | "string" | "The program to run, as the guest resolves it." |
      | "`args`" | "List[string]" | "Its arguments, one per element, passed without shell parsing." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Runs under the identity the handle carries: the agent identity on the
    handle a lab gives out, or the login `Machine.as_login` or
    `Machine.as_account` resolved. Output is streamed back and captured into
    an [ExecResult](ref_ws_types) with the exit code, stdout and stderr. The
    command is given 120 seconds; use `Machine.exec_timeout` for longer.
  DOC
  p <<DOC
    A non-zero exit code is not an error: it comes back in `exit_code`. The
    call fails when the agent is not reachable, when the guest cannot start
    the program, or when the timeout passes. Machines from templates built
    before the agent existed have no exec transport and fail here.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    match dc.exec("ipconfig", ["/all"]) {
        Ok(r) => { if r.exit_code == 0 { lab.log(r.stdout) } else { lab.log(r.stderr) } }
        Err(e) => lab.log("exec failed: " + e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.exec_timeout"
  p "`Machine.exec` with an explicit timeout."
  code wscript { source = "fn exec_timeout(self, cmd: string, args: List[string], timeout_secs: int) -> Result[ExecResult, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`cmd`" | "string" | "The program to run." |
      | "`args`" | "List[string]" | "Its arguments." |
      | "`timeout_secs`" | "int" | "How long the command may run, in seconds. Values below 1 are treated as 1." |
  }
  p "Identical to `Machine.exec` otherwise."
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(box) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let r = box.exec_timeout("apt-get", ["-y", "upgrade"], 1800)
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.copy_to"
  p "Copy a host file into the guest."
  code wscript { source = "fn copy_to(self, local: string, guest_path: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`local`" | "string" | "The host file. Relative paths resolve against the script's directory." |
      | "`guest_path`" | "string" | "Where to write it in the guest." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    Runs over the agent's file session under the handle's identity, so a
    handle from `Machine.as_login` writes into that login's home even before
    the account has ever logged on. The transfer is verified by digest. It
    fails when the agent is not reachable, when the host file cannot be
    read, or when the guest refuses the write.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.machine("api") else { return }
    let up = m.copy_to("conf/app.conf", "/etc/app.conf")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.copy_from"
  p "Copy a guest file to the host."
  code wscript { source = "fn copy_from(self, guest_path: string, local: string) -> Result[unit, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`guest_path`" | "string" | "The file in the guest." |
      | "`local`" | "string" | "Where to write it on the host. Relative paths resolve against the script's directory." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The mirror of `Machine.copy_to`. Missing parent directories on the host
    are created. It fails when the agent is not reachable, when the guest
    file cannot be read under the handle's identity, or when the host write
    fails.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn handle(event: Event, lab: Lab) {
    if event.name != "container.crashed" { return }
    let Ok(m) = lab.machine(event.vm) else { return }
    let down = m.copy_from("/var/log/app.log", "artefacts/app.log")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.logs"
  p "The last lines of the machine's console log."
  code wscript { source = "fn logs(self, lines: int) -> Result[string, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`lines`" | "int" | "How many lines from the end to return. A negative value is treated as 0." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    For a container this is its captured stdout and stderr with the
    micro-VM kernel log; for a VM it is the serial log. It fails with
    "machine <name> has no console log" where the machine keeps none, and
    with the read error otherwise.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(web) = lab.container("web") else { return }
    match web.logs(50) {
        Ok(text) => lab.log(text),
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.terminal"
  p "Open an interactive shell in the guest, driven send/expect style."
  code wscript { source = "fn terminal(self) -> Result[Term, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Opens one agent terminal session, 120 columns by 32 rows, running as the
    handle's identity, and returns a [Term](ref_ws_types) handle over it. The
    shell sees a real PTY, so prompts, echoes and escape sequences all
    arrive in the output buffer. Every call opens a new shell. Close it with
    `Term.close`. It fails when the agent is not reachable or lacks the
    terminal feature.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(vm) = lab.vm("box") else { return }
    let Ok(t) = vm.terminal() else { return }
    let s = t.send_line("hostname")
    let out = t.expect("box", 10)
    t.close()
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.logins"
  p "The logins the lab file declares for this machine."
  code wscript { source = "fn logins(self) -> List[Login]" }
  p <<DOC
    Returns one [Login](ref_ws_types) per `login {}` block, with the two
    implicit rules applied: a lone login is the default, and an undeclared
    `elevated` is true on Windows and false on Linux. The password crosses
    exactly as written, `None` where none was declared, so a provision
    creates the account the lab declares rather than a copy of it. It never
    fails; a machine with no logins returns an empty list. See
    [Logins and the SSH facade](guide_logins).
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    for login in dc.logins() {
        let Some(password) = login.password else { continue }
        let r = dc.exec("net", ["user", login.user, password, "/add"])
    }
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.as_login"
  p "A second handle onto the same machine whose guest work runs as a declared login."
  code wscript { source = "fn as_login(self, selector: string) -> Result[Machine, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`selector`" | "string" | "A `login {}` label, the account name as an alias, or the family floor `SYSTEM` or `root`, which resolves back to the agent identity." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The identity is resolved once, here, through the same resolution
    `vmlab exec --user` uses, so a script and the CLI cannot disagree about
    what a label names. Every method on the returned handle, `exec`,
    `copy_to`, `copy_from`, `terminal`, lands under that identity, which is
    how a `provision {}` step writes into the dev login's home before that
    user has ever logged on. The pointer position is shared with the
    original handle. It fails, loudly, when nothing matches the selector;
    that is what stops a provision from silently writing into the system
    profile. See [Logins and the SSH facade](guide_logins).
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let Ok(dev) = dc.as_login("dev") else { return }
    let c = dev.copy_to("settings.json", "%APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\settings.json")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.as_account"
  p "A second handle whose guest work runs as an account the lab file does not declare."
  code wscript { source = "fn as_account(self, user: string, password: string) -> Result[Machine, string]" }
  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`user`" | "string" | "The guest account, for example `PROBE\\audit`." |
      | "`password`" | "string" | "Its secret, or a rotated one for a declared account." |
  }
  p <<DOC
    The same as `Machine.as_login` with the secret supplied alongside, the
    pair `vmlab exec --user --password` takes. It fails when the guest
    rejects the pair or when the machine's guest family cannot mint a logon
    for it.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(dc) = lab.vm("dc01") else { return }
    let Ok(audit) = dc.as_account("PROBE\\audit", "s3cret") else { return }
    let r = audit.exec("whoami", [])
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Machine.stats"
  p "One sample of the guest's CPU, memory and disk usage."
  code wscript { source = "fn stats(self) -> Result[GuestStats, string]" }
  p <<DOC
    Asks the agent's metrics feature, waiting up to ten seconds, and returns
    a [GuestStats](ref_ws_types) record. It fails when the agent is not
    reachable, lacks the metrics feature, or does not answer in time.
  DOC
  code wscript { source = <<'SRC'
fn main(lab: Lab) {
    let Ok(m) = lab.machine("api") else { return }
    match m.stats() {
        Ok(s) => { for d in s.disks { lab.log(d.mount) } }
        Err(e) => lab.log(e),
    }
}
SRC
  }
}
